  {level:"B1",title:"Saturday Morning Markets",text:"Ten years ago, the car park behind our town hall was empty at weekends. Today, every Saturday morning, it becomes a busy market with more than forty stalls. Local farmers sell fresh vegetables, eggs and honey, while other sellers offer handmade soap, second-hand books and street food from around the world.\n\nThe market was started by a small group of residents who wanted people to buy local food instead of driving to the supermarket. At first, only six sellers came, and many people thought the idea would fail. However, the organisers advertised on social media and invited musicians to play, and slowly the crowds grew.\n\nNot everyone is happy, though. Some shop owners in the high street say the market takes customers away from them. The organisers disagree. They point out that many visitors stay in town after the market closes at one o'clock and have lunch in local cafés.",
   questions:[{q:"What was the car park like ten years ago at weekends?",options:["Full of cars","Empty","Used for concerts","Closed for repairs"],answer:1,explain:"It \"was empty at weekends\"."},
    {q:"Why did residents start the market?",options:["To raise money for the town hall","To encourage people to buy local food","To help musicians find work","To sell their old books"],answer:1,explain:"They wanted people to buy local food."},
    {q:"How many sellers came at the beginning?",options:["Four","Six","Ten","Forty"],answer:1,explain:"\"At first, only six sellers came.\""},
    {q:"What helped the market to grow?",options:["Lower prices","Free parking","Social media and live music","Support from supermarkets"],answer:2,explain:"They advertised on social media and invited musicians."},
    {q:"What do the organisers say about local cafés?",options:["They lose customers","They open later","They get more visitors after the market","They sell food at the market"],answer:2,explain:"Visitors stay in town and have lunch in local cafés."}]},
  {level:"B2",title:"The Case for Slow Travel",text:"For decades, the ideal holiday meant seeing as many places as possible: five cities in seven days, with a photo at every famous monument. Recently, however, a growing number of travellers have rejected this approach in favour of what is known as \"slow travel\".\n\nSlow travellers stay in one place for longer, often renting an apartment rather than a hotel room, and they try to live like locals. Instead of rushing between attractions, they shop at neighbourhood markets, learn a few phrases of the language and get to know the people who live there. Many choose trains or buses over flights, partly to reduce their carbon footprint and partly because, as one traveller put it, \"the journey is part of the holiday, not an obstacle to it\".\n\nCritics argue that slow travel is a luxury for those with plenty of free time and money. A two-week stay in a single village is not realistic for someone with only a few days of annual leave. Supporters respond that the principle can be applied to any trip: even on a weekend away, choosing one neighbourhood to explore properly is more rewarding than a rushed tour of a whole city.",
   questions:[{q:"What was the traditional idea of a good holiday?",options:["Staying in one hotel","Visiting many places quickly","Travelling by train","Living with a local family"],answer:1,explain:"Five cities in seven days — seeing as many places as possible."},
    {q:"Why do many slow travellers prefer trains or buses?",options:["They are always cheaper","They are faster than flights","For environmental reasons and because they enjoy the journey","Because flights are often cancelled"],answer:2,explain:"To reduce their carbon footprint and because the journey is part of the holiday."},
    {q:"What does \"an obstacle to it\" suggest about the journey?",options:["It is something in the way","It is the best part","It is dangerous","It is expensive"],answer:0,explain:"An obstacle is something that blocks you; the traveller disagrees with that view."},
    {q:"What is the critics' main point?",options:["Slow travel harms local people","Slow travel is boring","Not everyone has the time or money for it","It produces more pollution"],answer:2,explain:"It is \"a luxury for those with plenty of free time and money\"."},
    {q:"How do supporters answer the critics?",options:["They say everyone should take longer holidays","The idea can be used on short trips too","Governments should give more leave","Hotels should be cheaper"],answer:1,explain:"Even a weekend away can focus on one neighbourhood."}]},
  {level:"C1",title:"Too Much Choice?",text:"Conventional wisdom holds that more choice is always better: the wider the range of options, the more likely we are to find exactly what we want. Yet a substantial body of research suggests that, beyond a certain point, choice becomes a burden rather than a benefit.\n\nIn one frequently cited experiment, shoppers were offered samples of either six or twenty-four varieties of jam. The larger display attracted more attention, but customers who saw only six varieties were around ten times more likely to make a purchase. Psychologists attribute this to \"choice overload\": faced with too many alternatives, people find comparison exhausting, worry about making the wrong decision and frequently postpone choosing altogether.\n\nEven when people do decide, an abundance of options can undermine their satisfaction. The more alternatives we reject, the more easily we imagine that one of them might have been better — a phenomenon economists call opportunity cost. Paradoxically, then, those who insist on finding the very best option, sometimes labelled \"maximisers\", often end up less content than \"satisficers\", who settle for something that is simply good enough.\n\nNot all researchers accept these conclusions; some later studies failed to reproduce the jam effect. Nevertheless, many businesses have quietly reduced their product ranges, suggesting that the idea has at least some practical value.",
   questions:[{q:"What is the \"conventional wisdom\" mentioned at the start?",options:["Choice is a burden","More choice is always better","Shoppers prefer jam","Research is often wrong"],answer:1,explain:"Conventional wisdom: the more choice, the better."},
    {q:"In the jam experiment, which display led to more sales?",options:["The display with twenty-four jams","The display with six jams","Both sold the same","Neither display sold any jam"],answer:1,explain:"Customers who saw six varieties were ten times more likely to buy."},
    {q:"According to the text, why are \"maximisers\" often less satisfied?",options:["They spend too much money","They imagine rejected options might have been better","They choose too quickly","They dislike shopping"],answer:1,explain:"Rejecting many alternatives makes them imagine a better one."},
    {q:"What does \"settle for\" mean here?",options:["Pay for","Argue about","Accept something less than perfect","Look for something new"],answer:2,explain:"Satisficers accept something \"simply good enough\"."},
    {q:"What is the writer's view in the final paragraph?",options:["The research has been completely disproved","The idea is controversial but has some practical value","Businesses should offer more products","Only economists understand choice"],answer:1,explain:"Not all researchers agree, but businesses have found it useful."}]}
