  {level:"B2",title:"Is the Four-Day Week Here to Stay?",text:"When a software company in Manchester announced that its staff would work four days a week for the same salary, many observers predicted disaster. Two years later, the firm reports that productivity has risen by twelve per cent, sick days have halved and it has had no difficulty recruiting new employees.\n\nSimilar results have been reported in trials across several countries. Supporters argue that a shorter week forces teams to cut unnecessary meetings and focus on the work that matters most. They also point to improvements in mental health and a reduction in commuting, which benefits the environment.\n\nHowever, the model is not suitable for every business. Companies that must serve customers seven days a week, such as hospitals or retailers, cannot simply close on Fridays; instead, they need complex rotas, which can increase costs. Some employees have also reported feeling more pressure, as they try to complete five days of work in four.\n\nFor now, most experts agree that the four-day week works best when it is introduced gradually, with clear targets and regular reviews, rather than imposed overnight.",
   questions:[{q:"What happened to productivity at the Manchester company?",options:["It fell","It stayed the same","It rose by 12%","It doubled"],answer:2,explain:"\"Productivity has risen by twelve per cent.\""},
    {q:"According to supporters, why does a shorter week improve productivity?",options:["Staff are paid more","Teams cut unnecessary meetings and focus on key work","Offices are smaller","Managers check work more often"],answer:1,explain:"It forces teams to cut meetings and focus."},
    {q:"Why is the model difficult for hospitals and retailers?",options:["Their staff do not want it","They need to serve customers every day","They cannot afford computers","They have too few employees"],answer:1,explain:"They must serve customers seven days a week."},
    {q:"What problem have some employees reported?",options:["Lower salaries","Longer commutes","More pressure to finish work","Fewer holidays"],answer:2,explain:"They feel pressure to do five days of work in four."},
    {q:"What do most experts recommend?",options:["Introducing it gradually with targets and reviews","Introducing it immediately","Only for software companies","Avoiding it completely"],answer:0,explain:"Gradual introduction with clear targets and reviews."}]},
  {level:"C1",title:"Report extract: Customer retention",text:"Summary of findings\n\nOver the past financial year, the company lost approximately 18% of its subscription customers, compared with an industry average of 11%. While the number of new sign-ups remained strong, this level of churn significantly undermines long-term revenue growth, since acquiring a new customer costs roughly five times as much as retaining an existing one.\n\nExit surveys indicate that price was rarely the decisive factor. Instead, the most frequently cited reasons were slow responses from customer support and a perception that the product had \"stopped improving\". Notably, customers who contacted support more than twice in their first three months were almost three times as likely to cancel within a year.\n\nRecommendations\n\nWe recommend, first, that the company reallocate part of its marketing budget to expand the support team, with the aim of reducing average response times from 48 to 12 hours. Second, product updates should be communicated proactively, for example through short monthly newsletters, so that customers are aware of new features. Finally, a dedicated onboarding programme for new customers should be piloted, as early difficulties appear to be a strong predictor of later cancellation.",
   questions:[{q:"How does the company's churn compare with the industry?",options:["It is lower","It is about the same","It is significantly higher","The report does not say"],answer:2,explain:"18% compared with an industry average of 11%."},
    {q:"Why is churn described as a serious problem?",options:["New sign-ups are falling","Keeping customers is far cheaper than finding new ones","Prices are too low","The support team is too large"],answer:1,explain:"A new customer costs five times as much as retaining one."},
    {q:"What did exit surveys show about price?",options:["It was the main reason for leaving","It was rarely the decisive factor","Customers wanted higher prices","Prices were not mentioned"],answer:1,explain:"\"Price was rarely the decisive factor.\""},
    {q:"Which customers were most likely to cancel?",options:["Those who paid the most","Those who contacted support often early on","Those who joined through marketing","Those who read newsletters"],answer:1,explain:"Those who contacted support more than twice in the first three months."},
    {q:"Why does the report recommend an onboarding programme?",options:["Early difficulties predict later cancellation","It is required by law","Competitors already have one","It will reduce marketing costs"],answer:0,explain:"Early difficulties are a strong predictor of cancellation."}]}
